# **How to Use This Document**

This document contains the complete question bank for the Hive Enneagram Typing Engine. It is the authoritative reference for question content across all five stages. For the AI prompt, scoring logic, routing math, and JSON schema, see the companion document: Hive AI Prompt Specification v2.

Questions in this bank are grouped by stage. Each stage lists its format, purpose, and firing logic. Where answer options carry diagnostic signal, the framework axis, type signature, or correct answer is noted in parentheses for engine reference only — these markers are not shown to clients.

# **STAGE 0 · Warm-Up**

*Format: Open-ended text, one question per screen. AI processes all four responses together after Q4 is submitted.*

Purpose: Establish a baseline of the client's self-description in their own words. Used throughout the AI analysis as a language probe against the confirmed type's idealization and shadow patterns.

| **Q1 · SELF-DESCRIPTION** What are 3–5 words or phrases you would use to describe yourself? |
| --- |

| **Q2 · OTHERS****'**** DESCRIPTION** What are 3–5 words or phrases others would use to describe you? |
| --- |

| **Q3 · STRENGTH** Which of the words or phrases you listed is your greatest strength? *(Q1 and Q2 responses displayed for reference.)* |
| --- |

| **Q4 · SHADOW** Which of the words or phrases you listed tends to be most problematic for you? *(Q1 and Q2 responses displayed for reference.)* |
| --- |

# **STAGE 1 · Centers of Intelligence ****&**** Instincts**

*Format: Points assignment — client assigns up to 10 points across three responses. Unchanged from previous version. See engine specification for full question set.*

Stage 1 produces: (1) confirmed Center of Intelligence, (2) confirmed Instinct, (3) three type hypotheses to carry into Stage 2.

*Note: Stage 1 full question content (Centers, Instincts, Movement 1, Movement 2, and Lookalike Check) is maintained separately in the engine specification document along with scoring weights and routing logic.*

# **STAGE 2 · Cross-Referencing**

*Format: Multiple choice, single selection — client selects the response that feels most true. Three questions.*

Purpose: Narrow three type hypotheses from Stage 1 to a primary hypothesis and one live alternative, using three independent cross-framework lenses (Hornevian, Harmonic, Object Relations).

## **Q1 — Hornevian (Social Stance)**

| **What is your default strategy for getting what you want or need?** A — I step up and go for it — I'm direct, sometimes forceful, and I trust myself to make things happen. (Assertive: 3, 7, 8) B — I work with others carefully — I try to do the right thing, follow the right process, or earn it through effort and relationship. (Compliant: 1, 2, 6) C — I pull back and figure out my approach first — I need time to think, feel, or map things out before I move. (Withdrawn: 4, 5, 9) |
| --- |

## **Q2 — Harmonic (Conflict Response)**

| **How do you react when things don****'****t go the way you were expecting?** A — I call it out directly — I feel it intensely and I want to deal with it immediately. (Intensity: 4, 6, 8) B — I try to look on the bright side — I'd rather find what's still working than dwell on what went wrong. (Positive: 2, 7, 9) C — I go into problem-solving mode — I want to understand what happened and figure out how to fix it. (Competency: 1, 3, 5) |
| --- |

## **Q3 — Object Relations (Life Theme)**

| **Which of these has been a quiet but consistent theme over the arc of your life, even when things are otherwise going well?** A — Needing to feel genuinely connected and knowing where I stand — with people, in relationships, or in the structures and groups that matter to me. (Attachment: 3, 6, 9) B — A sense that things — relationships, experiences, or life itself — rarely quite live up to what they could or should be. (Frustration: 1, 4, 7) C — A quiet discomfort with feeling too exposed, too dependent, or too vulnerable with others. (Rejection: 2, 5, 8) |
| --- |

## **Cross-Referencing Signature Table**

*Every type has a unique three-answer combination. No two types share the same pattern.*

| **Type** | **Q1 Hornevian** | **Q2 Harmonic** | **Q3 Object Relations** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Compliant | Competency | Frustration |
| 2 | Compliant | Positive | Rejection |
| 3 | Assertive | Competency | Attachment |
| 4 | Withdrawn | Intensity | Frustration |
| 5 | Withdrawn | Competency | Rejection |
| 6 | Compliant | Intensity | Attachment |
| 7 | Assertive | Positive | Frustration |
| 8 | Assertive | Intensity | Rejection |
| 9 | Withdrawn | Positive | Attachment |

# **STAGE 3 · Pairwise Discrimination**

*Purpose: Confirm the primary hypothesis from Stage 2 against the one live alternative. Two modes: Standard Mode and Counter-Type Mode.*

| **Stems** **Standard Mode Q1: **"When you're at your best, how would you describe your internal experience?" **Standard Mode Q2 (high-ambiguity pairs only): **"Which of these feels most uncomfortable or intolerable when it shows up in your life?" High-ambiguity pairs (Q2 always fires): 1v6, 1v9, 2v6, 2v9, 3v7, 3v8, 4v5, 4v9, 5v9, 6v8 Lower-numbered type is always Person A. Response options: Person A / Both but more A / Both but more B / Person B. |
| --- |

## **Routing Logic**

| **Decision 1 — Standard Mode or Counter-Type Mode?** Check: Does Stage 1 instinct + Stage 2 primary type = known counter-type combination? YES → Counter-Type Mode: Fire single CT question. One question only. Done. NO → Standard Mode: Proceed to Decision 2. |
| --- |

| **Decision 2 — Single or Double Question? (Standard Mode only)** Check: Is the surviving pair one of the 10 high-ambiguity pairings? YES (high-ambiguity pair) → Fire Q1 + Q2 regardless of Q1 result. NO → Fire Q1 only. If answer is 'Both' → fire Q2 conditionally. |
| --- |

## **Counter-Type Trigger Table**

| **Instinct** | **Type** | **Counter-Type** | **Question Fired** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| SO | 7 | SO 7 (Sacrifice) | CT-1: SO 7 vs. Type 2 |
| SX | 6 | SX 6 (Counterphobic) | CT-2: SX 6 vs. Type 8 |
| SP | 3 | SP 3 (Anti-Vanity) | CT-3: SP 3 vs. Type 1 |
| SP | 4 | SP 4 (Tenacity) | CT-4: SP 4 vs. Type 3 |
| SX | 1 | SX 1 (Zeal) | CT-5: SX 1 vs. Type 8 |

## **Stage 3 Q1 — Core Motivation (All Pairings)**

Question stem: "When you're at your best, how would you describe your internal experience?" Format: Person A / Both but more A / Both but more B / Person B. Lower-numbered type always Person A.

### **The Nine Motivational Descriptions (Library)**

*These nine descriptions are assembled into pairs dynamically. Each description appears in multiple pairings.*

| **Type 1** I am doing things the right way. I feel principled, clear, and in integrity with my own standards. |
| --- |

| **Type 2** I am tuned in to what others need. I feel genuinely helpful, warm, and deeply connected. |
| --- |

| **Type 3** I am achieving something meaningful. I feel capable, successful, and recognized for what I bring. |
| --- |

| **Type 4** I am fully and authentically myself. I feel deeply seen, creatively alive, and emotionally real. |
| --- |

| **Type 5** I understand what's happening at a deep level. I feel clear, self-sufficient, and completely capable. |
| --- |

| **Type 6** I am prepared and loyal to what matters. I feel reliable, certain, and securely connected to people I trust. |
| --- |

| **Type 7** I am experiencing life to the fullest. I feel free, expansive, and open to everything available to me. |
| --- |

| **Type 8** I am fully in control of my world. I feel strong, powerful, and completely unbothered by outside pressure. |
| --- |

| **Type 9** Everything feels settled and at peace. I feel harmonious, easy, and genuinely okay with where things are. |
| --- |

